Narrator: That evening, Catherine finds Marloch sitting beside the lake. Almost tired.
Narrator: He looks less threatening than before.
join Marloch center
Marloch: You came back.
catherine: So did you.
Marloch: I live here.
catherine: Do you?
Marloch: What sort of question is that?  it’s the least of pleasantries, yes, I’m not from here. You came all this way for nothing.
catherine: Thank you for reminding me…
Marloch: The universe can be unfair from time to time, I’m sorry
Marloch: You seem disappointed. Is it because I refused to help you?
catherine: Because I thought the stories were true. Perhaps that is what hurts most.
catherine: My mother spent years telling me about this place.
Marloch: And what did she say?
catherine: That you were kind and powerful and understanding and.. whatever. Not like this.
Marloch: Your mother was wrong.
catherine: I don’t think she was, at least some of it is true. I believe you weren’t like someone bad before. Not as bad as you might look to be. I also don’t think you enjoyed becoming what you are.
Marloch: You know nothing about me.
catherine: No. But I know what it is like to be trapped inside something you never chose.
catherine: Catherine's eyes begin to water.m I'm afraid. I am afraid of dying
I still want to live. I want to travel. I want to see things. I want to be old and complain about my knees.
Marloch: The universe can be unfair from time to time.
set {EndingTracking.marloch_feeling} += 1.0
